---
title: "On Imposter Syndrome"
subtitle: "Fitting in until you feel comfortable where you are"
---
There are many times in life where you're going to feel uncomfortable, or where you feel like an outsider, where you feel like you need to justify your place to yourself and the people around you.

This is OK. It's common. It will happen, and if it's not something that is happening naturally, you need to make it happen because it shows that you're progressing. This happens every time your world changes. For all changes, small and big. This will happen when you go from a shitty school to one for talented people. It'll happen when you move from high school into college, or from undergrad to graduate school. From a small company to a large one, or to a well funded elite team.

With every step forward you take, you'll be surrounded with many new people, in different surroundings, and there is an accompanying adjustment period. And it's not just school or work, but it's going from just being around different people. From incompetent to competent, from poor or working class, to wealthy.

You might feel like you don't fit in – that's where you have to try to fit in until you do. You have to have a reason for yourself why you belong there or higher. You need to tell people why you should be there as well after convincing yourself. Because if you don't know why you are somewhere, you'll get tired very quickly. It will wear you out the quickest when you're conscious of yourself and others.

To make it natural, force the naturalness until it is. Then climb higher and do it again.

Even if it's luck that got you there and you know and are conscious of that, cling to it. Make it natural and your place will become natural as over time you carve out a reason for your being where you are.

And you'll do this process again, and again, and again as you progress through life.

Maybe you'll find it fun. It's better if you find it fun. Because no matter what, you have to do it. So you may as well try to find it fun.
